        title: 'Product Overview',
        content:
          'The Interactive Task Wall is built on a straightforward observation about exhibition floors: people will not read your wall, but they will absolutely try to beat it. By converting a large branded surface into a grid of illuminated, pressure-responsive buttons, the wall gives visitors something to attempt rather than something to consume. A challenge is launched, a timer starts, buttons light in sequence or in pattern, and the visitor moves, presses, thinks, and scores while everyone standing behind them watches the clock run down.\n\nThe challenge library is what makes the wall versatile. A reaction round lights random buttons that must be hit before they fade, testing speed and accuracy. A memory round plays a sequence the visitor must repeat, escalating in length until they fail. A knowledge round poses a question on screen with answer options mapped to buttons, embedding your product facts, safety rules, scheme details, or campaign messages directly into the game. A team round splits the wall into zones for two players competing side by side. Nebuloid selects and tunes the mix based on your audience and the message you need to survive the interaction.\n\nMessage integration is deliberate rather than decorative. In most gamified activations the brand is merely wallpaper around a generic game. Here the questions, the visual states, the win conditions, and the post-game screen are written around your content, so a visitor who plays the wall for ninety seconds has physically engaged with your key facts several times over. Repetition through play produces retention that a poster or a looping video simply cannot match, and the leaderboard motivates visitors to play again, which multiplies the exposure.\n\nPhysically, the wall is fabricated as a designed architectural element. Panels are built to your stand or venue dimensions, buttons are recessed into a rigid faceplate with individually addressable illumination, and the surrounding graphics, lighting, and screen are integrated so the installation reads as a single object. Button hardware is industrial grade, mounted with reinforced backing to absorb enthusiastic strikes, and wiring is routed into a managed controller so a single faulty switch can be replaced in minutes without dismantling the wall.\n\nThroughput and operations are engineered alongside the game design. Rounds are short, usually thirty to sixty seconds, and the wall resets itself the moment a round ends. A host with a tablet can capture the player name, choose a challenge, adjust difficulty, and clear the board, while the leaderboard on screen continues to run during idle periods with a standing invitation to beat the current champion. Optional lead capture attaches a name, company, and contact to each score, turning the queue into a qualified list that syncs to your CRM.\n\nEvery round generates data. The platform records attempts, scores, completion rates, difficulty distribution, question-level accuracy, repeat play, and hourly demand. Question accuracy is especially valuable, because it tells you precisely which of your messages the audience already understands and which ones they consistently get wrong, which is a direct input into how you brief your sales team and write your next campaign.',
